Implementation sketch:

```ts
function moduleSourceFiles(session: Session, qualifier: string): ts.SourceFile[] {
  const found = new Map<string, ts.SourceFile>();
  const checker = session.project.checker;
  for (const file of session.project.files) {
    const visit = (node: ts.Node): void => {
      let name: ts.Identifier | undefined;
      if (ts.isNamespaceExport(node)) name = node.name;
      else if (ts.isNamespaceImport(node)) name = node.name;
      if (name && name.text === qualifier) {
        const symbol = checker.getSymbolAtLocation(name);
        const target = symbol && (symbol.flags & ts.SymbolFlags.Alias) ? checker.getAliasedSymbol(symbol) : symbol;
        for (const declaration of target?.declarations ?? []) {
          const source = ts.isSourceFile(declaration) ? declaration : undefined;
          if (source) found.set(source.fileName, source);
        }
      }
      ts.forEachChild(node, visit);
    };
    visit(file);
  }
  return [...found.values()];
}
```

Also handle `import { Permission } from ...` where Permission is a module? Could add: any identifier matching qualifier, resolve alias to symbol whose declaration is SourceFile. But iterating every identifier could be costly but fine. Simpler: for each file, visit identifiers named qualifier; get symbol; alias-resolve; if declarations include SourceFile, add. That covers namespace export, namespace import, and `import Permission = require(...)`. However, an identifier `Permission` used as a value of a namespace import would also resolve to module symbol. That's exactly what we want. But careful: `import { Permission } from "./permission"` where Permission is a service object, not module; its symbol declarations are variable, not SourceFile, so ignored.

What if qualifier `Permission` also is a local variable somewhere unrelated, but one occurrence resolves to module? We add module. Fine.

But only resolve identifiers in source files; there might be many named Permission (e.g. string). Filter ts.isIdentifier; getSymbolAtLocation on every matching identifier. Fine.

Then:

```ts
function qualifiedMatches(session: Session, query: string): Node[] | undefined {
  const parts = query.split(".");
  if (parts.length < 2) return undefined;
  const qualifier = parts[0]!;
  const memberName = parts[parts.length - 1]!;
  const modules = moduleSourceFiles(session, qualifier);
  if (!modules.length) return undefined;
  const found = new Map<string, Node>();
  const add = (declaration: ts.Declaration) => {
    const node = nodeOf(session.project, declaration);
    found.set(keyOf(node), node);
  };
  for (const source of modules) {
    const visit = (node: ts.Node): void => {
      const name = (node as {name?: ts.Node}).name;
      if (name && propertyNameText(name) === memberName && isNamedMember(node)) {
        const member = node as ts.Declaration;
        add(member);
        for (const impl of lookupImplementation(session.project, member).implementations) add(impl);
      }
      ts.forEachChild(node, visit);
    };
    visit(source);
  }
  return [...found.values()];
}
```

What counts as isNamedMember? To avoid matching e.g. a function parameter named assert (irrelevant). Declaration-ish:
- FunctionDeclaration, ClassDeclaration? For query member, class? interface/class property/method etc.
- VariableDeclaration (const assert) - yes
- PropertyAssignment (in object literal) - maybe implementation; but lookupImplementation on property assignment? Let's think. We can just include VariableDeclaration, FunctionDeclaration, MethodDeclaration, PropertyDeclaration, MethodSignature, PropertySignature, GetAccessor/SetAccessor. Skip PropertyAssignment unless object literal is implementation. But layer's `Service.of({ assert })` is shorthand, not property assignment. Fine.

For members that are interface members, lookupImplementation adds impls. For variable declarations, lookupImplementation returns [declaration] as definition; adding it again dedupes. For method/class etc.

Should we add implementations for *any* member or only interface/class type members? `lookupImplementation` for a variable: isConcreteImplementation VariableDeclaration -> [declaration]. For FunctionDeclaration -> [declaration]. Fine. For PropertyAssignment -> isConcreteImplementation false, ownerTypeSymbol? parent object literal no -> [] and returns []. So safe.

But there's a subtlety: `lookupImplementation` calls implementationsOfMember which uses writtenObjectsOf across all project files, and for interface Interface.assert it finds the const assert. Good.

